// Part of the MIND project (Machine Intelligence Native Design).
//
// WHAT THIS DEMONSTRATES, AND WHY IT IS DIFFERENT FROM THE OTHER QUANT
// EXAMPLES.  `examples/quant/black_scholes` prices the determinism claim in a
// unit a risk officer reads without translation.  `greeks_higher_order` pushes
// the same claim two derivatives deeper, into the place where floating-point
// error compounds worst.  This file does something neither of those can: it
// prices an instrument whose value is a DIFFERENCE OF LARGE, NEARLY EQUAL
// TERMS, and which therefore carries an exact structural identity that the
// arithmetic must satisfy to full precision no matter what any oracle says.
//
// A barrier price is assembled from the Reiner-Rubinstein building blocks as
// combinations like `A - B + C - D`.  At a barrier far from spot those four
// terms are each of order the vanilla premium while their signed sum is of
// order 1e-5, or 1e-27, or 1e-117.  The U_FAR case below has a knock-in value
// of 5.4e-27 formed by cancelling terms of order 10: that is twenty-six orders
// of magnitude of cancellation in a single expression.  Nothing in the other
// quant examples asks the kernel for that.
//
// AND THE PAYOFF FOR IT IS THE IN-OUT PARITY:
//     knock_in(X) + knock_out(X) == vanilla(X),   exactly, for every X
// because a contract that pays if the barrier is touched plus one that pays if
// it is not is just the unconditional contract.  This is a THEOREM, not a
// calibration.  It holds independently of r, q, sigma, T, S, K and H; it holds
// independently of whether my transcription of Reiner-Rubinstein is right; and
// it is violated by essentially every sign error, every swapped eta, and every
// mis-assembled branch of the eight-way type table.  It is the single most
// valuable check in this file, and unlike a tolerance band against a reference
// number it cannot be satisfied by a wrong implementation that happens to be
// wrong consistently.  See KAT section 2.
//
// A FINDING THAT SHAPED THIS FILE, RECORDED BECAUSE IT IS THE INTERESTING PART.
// While validating the closed forms at 50 digits, the formulas were evaluated
// with the barrier on the WRONG SIDE of spot — a "down" barrier placed above
// the spot price, which means the contract has ALREADY knocked at inception.
// The formulas returned, in full 50-digit glory:
//     down-and-out call,  S=100, H=120:  -55.19    (a negative option price)
//     down-and-in  call,  S=100, H=1000:  313230.17 (30000x the vanilla)
// and IN-OUT PARITY HELD TO 1e-49 ON BOTH.  That is the whole lesson in one
// line: the identity this file leans on is a real theorem and it is still not
// sufficient, because a decomposition can be internally consistent and
// economically meaningless at the same time.  Parity constrains the SUM of the
// two halves; it says nothing about whether either half is a price.
//
// The fix is a DOMAIN GUARD, not a wider tolerance.  A down barrier requires
// H < S and an up barrier requires H > S; outside that the contract has
// already triggered and the closed form is answering a question about a
// contract that no longer exists.  `bar_valid_down` / `bar_valid_up` return
// the canonical qNaN there rather than a confident negative number.  KAT
// section 5 asserts exactly that, and the adversarial block in section 6
// asserts that the parity check CANNOT catch it — a negative control on the
// file's own headline identity.
//
// WHY THIS IS NOT A LIBRARY.  The formulas are standard and unremarkable
// (Merton 1973 for the down-and-out call; Reiner & Rubinstein 1991 for the
// complete eight-type table).  Nothing here is a contribution to finance.  The
// contribution is that every transcendental on the path — exp, log, sqrt, erfc
// — is rebuilt from `+ - * /` on the strict scalar path, so a barrier premium
// is reproducible bit-for-bit on a different ISA rather than merely close.
//
// THE CLAIM, STATED EXACTLY.  This module does NOT claim to match any host
// libm, SciPy, or a vendor pricing library in the last bits.  It cannot, and it
// is not trying to: a re-derived erfc rounds differently from Cephes, and both
// are within their stated accuracy.  The claim is narrower and harder:
//     the same source, compiled for two different ISAs, produces byte-identical
//     output, with a stated accuracy bound against a 50-digit oracle.
// Accuracy and reproducibility are orthogonal properties.  Determinism is what
// makes an error analysis meaningful, not a substitute for one.
//
// REBATE IS ZERO, AND WHY THAT IS A SCOPE DECISION RATHER THAN AN OMISSION.
// The full Reiner-Rubinstein decomposition has six blocks, A through F.  E and
// F exist ONLY to price a rebate — the cash paid when a knock-out is
// extinguished (F) or when a knock-in never activates (E).  With a zero rebate
// both are multiplied by zero and contribute nothing to any of the eight
// prices below, so they are not implemented.  That is deliberate: an
// unexercised E and F would be code the KAT cannot reach, and untested code
// inside a file whose entire purpose is to be a known-answer test is worse
// than absent code.  A non-zero rebate also needs the `lambda` exponent and
// a second pair of x/y arguments, which would roughly double the surface for a
// feature no check here would constrain.
// deferred: rebate support (blocks E and F, plus the lambda exponent) is out of
// scope — upgrade path: add `rr_e`/`rr_f` taking the rebate K, extend each of
// the eight assemblies with its rebate term per Reiner-Rubinstein Table 2, and
// extend the KAT with a rebate sweep whose limit as rebate -> 0 reproduces
// every constant in this file.
//
// SELF-CONTAINED, AND WHY.  The detmath kernels are duplicated below instead of
// imported.  mindc 0.10.2 cannot carry an f64 across a module boundary (roadmap
// 17.1): an imported `fn f(x: f64) -> f64` types as `(i64) -> i64` at the call
// site and miscompiles silently.  This mirrors `examples/detmath_kat`,
// `examples/quant/black_scholes` and `examples/quant/greeks_higher_order`.  The
// kernel block is copied BYTE-IDENTICALLY from black_scholes so the two
// examples cannot silently drift to different kernels and disagree on a price.
// deferred: replace the duplicated kernel block with `import std.detmath;` once
// roadmap 17.1 lands — upgrade path: delete the block between the KERNEL BEGIN
// and KERNEL END markers and call the std surface directly.
//
// KERNELS ARE NAMED `dm_*` DELIBERATELY.  A kernel named `exp`, `log`, `pow`
// or `erfc` collides with the C99 libm name and can be constant-folded by the
// host toolchain at a call site with literal arguments, silently substituting
// the platform's rounding for ours — which is the exact bug this whole example
// exists to rule out.  The `dm_` prefix makes that collision impossible.  Note
// that this file needs a POWER function (the `(H/S)^(2*mu)` factors), and it is
// named `dm_powf` for precisely this reason: a MIND function named `pow` is a
// live miscompilation hazard, not a hypothetical one.
//
// NO `[test]` FUNCTIONS, AND WHY.  The mindc 0.10.2 `[test]` interpreter
// mis-evaluates an f64 comparison in a function body (it takes the then-branch
// unconditionally).  Every guarded function here would return through its
// leading `if x != x` guard and every interpreted assertion would pass
// vacuously — a false green.  The native backend evaluates f64 branches
// correctly, so the gate is build-and-run: `mindc run`, exit code 0 means every
// check passed.  The count of failures is the exit code, so a partial failure
// is never rounded to success.
//
// REFERENCES (original papers only):
//   Merton, R. C. (1973). "Theory of Rational Option Pricing." Bell Journal of
//     Economics and Management Science 4(1), 141-183.  — the down-and-out call,
//     the first closed-form barrier price.
//   Reiner, E. & Rubinstein, M. (1991). "Breaking Down the Barriers." Risk 4(8),
//     28-35. — the complete (phi, eta) parameterisation used here.
//   Black, F. & Scholes, M. (1973). "The Pricing of Options and Corporate
//     Liabilities." Journal of Political Economy 81(3), 637-654.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// =========================== KERNEL BEGIN ==================================
// Duplicated verbatim from std/detmath.mind (roadmap 17.1 blocks importing).
// Do not "improve" these: every polynomial is a fixed left-fold Horner
// recurrence in source order.  Reassociating one changes the last bit of every
// price downstream and breaks the cross-substrate hash.

fn absf(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x < 0.0 {
        return 0.0 - x;
    }
    return x;
}

// +inf by byte-identical IEEE overflow; never a synthesised NaN.
fn inf() -> f64 {
    let mut v: f64 = 1.0;
    let mut i: i64 = 0;
    while i < 350 {
        v = v * 10.0;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// 0x7FF8000000000000 — the MIND-canonical quiet NaN, pinned so an
// out-of-domain result is byte-identical (x86 sets the sign bit, ARM does not).
fn canonical_qnan() -> f64 {
    return __mind_bits_to_f64(9221120237041090560);
}

fn rnd_to_i64(x: f64) -> i64 {
    if x >= 0.0 {
        return (x + 0.5) as i64;
    }
    return 0 - ((0.5 - x) as i64);
}

fn det_exp(x: f64) -> f64 {
    let ln2_hi: f64 = 0.6931467056274414;
    let ln2_lo: f64 = 0.00000047493250390941725;
    let inv_ln2: f64 = 1.4426950408889634;
    let k: i64 = rnd_to_i64(x * inv_ln2);
    let kf: f64 = k as f64;
    let r: f64 = (x - kf * ln2_hi) - kf * ln2_lo;
    let mut t: f64 = 0.0000000000007647163731819816;
    t = 0.00000000016059043836821613 + r * t;
    t = 0.00000000208767569878681 + r * t;
    t = 0.00000002505210838544172 + r * t;
    t = 0.0000002755731922398589 + r * t;
    t = 0.0000027557319223985893 + r * t;
    t = 0.0000248015873015873 + r * t;
    t = 0.0001984126984126984 + r * t;
    t = 0.001388888888888889 + r * t;
    t = 0.008333333333333333 + r * t;
    t = 0.041666666666666664 + r * t;
    t = 0.16666666666666666 + r * t;
    t = 0.5 + r * t;
    t = 1.0 + r * t;
    t = 1.0 + r * t;
    let mut y: f64 = t * 1.0;
    let mut j: i64 = 0;
    if k >= 0 {
        while j < k {
            y = y * 2.0;
            j = j + 1;
        }
    } else {
        while j < (0 - k) {
            y = y / 2.0;
            j = j + 1;
        }
    }
    return y;
}

fn dm_exp(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    if x >= 709.7827128933841 {
        return inf();
    }
    if x <= 0.0 - 745.1332191019412 {
        return 0.0;
    }
    return det_exp(x);
}

fn det_log_core(x: f64) -> f64 {
    let ln2: f64 = 0.6931471805599453;
    let mut k: i64 = 0;
    // f64-param-init: seed a loop-carried f64 binding via the exact identity
    // `x * 1.0` so mindc 0.10.2 does not infer it as i64.
    let mut m: f64 = x * 1.0;
    while m >= 1.4142135623730951 {
        m = m / 2.0;
        k = k + 1;
    }
    while m < 0.7071067811865476 {
        m = m * 2.0;
        k = k - 1;
    }
    let s: f64 = (m - 1.0) / (m + 1.0);
    let s2: f64 = s * s;
    let mut acc: f64 = 0.0;
    let mut n: i64 = 29;
    while n >= 1 {
        acc = 1.0 / (n as f64) + s2 * acc;
        n = n - 2;
    }
    return (k as f64) * ln2 + 2.0 * s * acc;
}

fn dm_log(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    if x < 0.0 {
        return 0.0;
    }
    if x == 0.0 {
        return 0.0 - inf();
    }
    if x >= inf() {
        return inf();
    }
    return det_log_core(x);
}

fn dm_sqrt(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if x < 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    return sqrt(x);
}

// erf by its Maclaurin series, ascending fixed left-fold.  Certified |x| < 2.
fn erf_series(x: f64) -> f64 {
    let two_over_sqrtpi: f64 = 1.1283791670955126;
    let x2: f64 = x * x;
    let mut t: f64 = x * 1.0;
    let mut s: f64 = x * 1.0;
    let mut n: i64 = 1;
    while n < 60 {
        t = t * (0.0 - x2) / (n as f64);
        s = s + t / ((2 * n + 1) as f64);
        n = n + 1;
    }
    return two_over_sqrtpi * s;
}

// erfc by a fixed-depth backward continued fraction.  Certified x >= 1.
// The depth is static, never convergence-tested: a convergence test would be
// deterministic but would make the rounding sequence input-dependent.
fn erfc_cf(x: f64) -> f64 {
    let sqrtpi: f64 = 1.7724538509055159;
    let mut acc: f64 = 0.0;
    let mut n: i64 = 200;
    while n >= 1 {
        acc = ((n as f64) * 0.5) / (x + acc);
        n = n - 1;
    }
    return det_exp(0.0 - x * x) / sqrtpi / (x + acc);
}

fn dm_erfc(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    if x >= inf() {
        return 0.0;
    }
    if x <= 0.0 - inf() {
        return 2.0;
    }
    if x < 0.0 {
        return 2.0 - dm_erfc(0.0 - x);
    }
    if x < 1.0 {
        return 1.0 - erf_series(x);
    }
    return erfc_cf(x);
}

// Standard normal CDF.  Computed as 0.5*erfc(-x/sqrt(2)), NEVER as the
// reflected 1 - norm_cdf(-x): the reflected form cancels to exactly 0.0 in the
// left tail, which is precisely where a deep out-of-the-money option lives.
// See the deep-OTM KAT below, where the true call premium is 4.955e-12 and a
// reflected implementation would report a worthless option as exactly
// worthless, with no indication of error.
fn dm_norm_cdf(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    let inv_sqrt2: f64 = 0.7071067811865476;
    return 0.5 * dm_erfc(0.0 - x * inv_sqrt2);
}

// Standard normal PDF, phi(x) = exp(-x^2/2)/sqrt(2*pi).  Needed by gamma,
// vega and theta.  1/sqrt(2*pi) is a rounded constant, not a division.
fn dm_norm_pdf(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    let inv_sqrt_2pi: f64 = 0.3989422804014327;
    return inv_sqrt_2pi * det_exp(0.0 - 0.5 * x * x);
}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Deterministic real power, x^y for x > 0.
//
// NAMED `dm_powf`, NOT `pow`.  A MIND function named `pow` matches the C99
// libm symbol and can be captured by the host toolchain and constant-folded to
// the platform's `pow` at any call site with literal arguments, silently
// replacing this body.  That is not a hypothetical: it is a documented defect
// class in this repo, and it is why every kernel here carries the `dm_` prefix.
//
// Built as exp(y * log(x)) on the deterministic kernels rather than by a
// dedicated algorithm.  This is the accuracy-honest choice AND the
// determinism-honest one: it composes two functions whose rounding is already
// pinned, instead of introducing a third independent approximation whose error
// would have to be characterised separately.  It costs roughly one extra ulp
// per operand versus a fused implementation, which is inside every band below
// — and the barrier exponents `2*mu` and `2*(mu+1)` are the one place in this
// file where the input to the power is a ratio near 1, so the log is
// well-conditioned exactly where it is used.
//
// Only x > 0 is supported, which is all the barrier formulas ever ask for:
// the base is always H/S, a ratio of two positive prices.  A non-positive base
// returns the canonical qNaN rather than a plausible number.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn dm_powf(x: f64, y: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if y != y {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if x <= 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    return dm_exp(y * dm_log(x));
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Black-Scholes-Merton vanilla scaffolding with a continuous dividend yield q.
//
// These are duplicated from `examples/quant/black_scholes` and are NOT the
// deliverable of this module.  They are here because the in-out parity check —
// the headline identity of this file — needs a vanilla price to compare
// against, and computing that vanilla price with the SAME kernels as the
// barrier prices is what makes the comparison meaningful.  A parity check
// against an externally supplied vanilla constant would be a value check
// wearing an identity's clothes: it would fold the kernel's own error into the
// residual and force a loose band, destroying the very tightness that makes
// parity worth asserting.  Computed in-file, parity holds to ~1e-13 absolute
// on prices of order 10, which is near machine precision.
//
// EVALUATION ORDER IS PART OF THE CONTRACT.  d1 is formed exactly as in
// `examples/quant/black_scholes`, with the numerator summed left to right
// before the single division.  Regrouping it is algebraically identical and
// numerically different, and would change the last bit of every price below.
// Do not "simplify" these expressions.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn bs_valid(s: f64, k: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> i64 {
    if s != s {
        return 0;
    }
    if k != k {
        return 0;
    }
    if sigma != sigma {
        return 0;
    }
    if t != t {
        return 0;
    }
    if s <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if k <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if sigma <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if t <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}

fn bs_d1(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    let sq: f64 = sigma * dm_sqrt(t);
    return (dm_log(s / k) + (r - q + 0.5 * sigma * sigma) * t) / sq;
}

fn bs_d2(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    return bs_d1(s, k, r, q, sigma, t) - sigma * dm_sqrt(t);
}

pub fn bs_call(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if bs_valid(s, k, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let d1: f64 = bs_d1(s, k, r, q, sigma, t);
    let d2: f64 = d1 - sigma * dm_sqrt(t);
    let df_r: f64 = dm_exp(0.0 - r * t);
    let df_q: f64 = dm_exp(0.0 - q * t);
    return s * df_q * dm_norm_cdf(d1) - k * df_r * dm_norm_cdf(d2);
}

pub fn bs_put(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if bs_valid(s, k, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let d1: f64 = bs_d1(s, k, r, q, sigma, t);
    let d2: f64 = d1 - sigma * dm_sqrt(t);
    let df_r: f64 = dm_exp(0.0 - r * t);
    let df_q: f64 = dm_exp(0.0 - q * t);
    return k * df_r * dm_norm_cdf(0.0 - d2) - s * df_q * dm_norm_cdf(0.0 - d1);
}

// ===========================================================================
// THE REINER-RUBINSTEIN DECOMPOSITION.
//
// Continuously-monitored single-barrier options, zero rebate.  Every one of the
// eight prices is a signed combination of four building blocks A, B, C, D,
// each parameterised by two sign flags:
//
//     phi = +1 for a CALL payoff, -1 for a PUT payoff
//     eta = +1 for a DOWN barrier, -1 for an UP barrier
//
// The single (phi, eta) parameterisation is the reason this file is short
// rather than eight transcriptions of eight formulas.  It is also the reason a
// single swapped eta breaks four prices at once and is caught immediately by
// the parity check — a benefit of shared structure that eight independent
// transcriptions would not have.
//
// The common quantities:
//     mu     = (r - q - sigma^2/2) / sigma^2
//     sigt   = sigma * sqrt(T)
//     x1 = log(S/K)/sigt     + (1+mu)*sigt
//     x2 = log(S/H)/sigt     + (1+mu)*sigt
//     y1 = log(H^2/(S*K))/sigt + (1+mu)*sigt
//     y2 = log(H/S)/sigt     + (1+mu)*sigt
//
// and the blocks themselves:
//     A = phi*S*e^(-qT)*N(phi*x1) - phi*K*e^(-rT)*N(phi*x1 - phi*sigt)
//     B = phi*S*e^(-qT)*N(phi*x2) - phi*K*e^(-rT)*N(phi*x2 - phi*sigt)
//     C = phi*S*e^(-qT)*(H/S)^(2(mu+1))*N(eta*y1)
//         - phi*K*e^(-rT)*(H/S)^(2mu)*N(eta*y1 - eta*sigt)
//     D = phi*S*e^(-qT)*(H/S)^(2(mu+1))*N(eta*y2)
//         - phi*K*e^(-rT)*(H/S)^(2mu)*N(eta*y2 - eta*sigt)
//
// A is the vanilla premium.  C and D are its image under the reflection that
// implements the method of images for the absorbing boundary at H; the
// `(H/S)^(2mu)` factors are the Girsanov weight that reflection carries.
//
// WHY EACH BLOCK IS ITS OWN FUNCTION rather than one function returning four
// values: mindc 0.10.2 has no tuple return, and more importantly this keeps
// each block independently readable against the paper.  The cost is that the
// shared sub-expressions (mu, sigt, the x/y arguments) are recomputed per
// block.  That is deliberate and must NOT be "optimised" by hoisting them into
// a shared mutable scratch: recomputation is the only way to guarantee each
// block sees the identical rounding of each sub-expression regardless of call
// order, which is exactly the property the cross-substrate hash depends on.
//
// CANCELLATION IS THE NORMAL CASE HERE, NOT THE EDGE CASE.  Look at U_FAR in
// the KAT: H = 1000 against S = 100, and the up-and-in call is 5.4e-27 while A
// and B are both ~10.45.  The assembled expression `B - C + D` must cancel
// twenty-six significant digits' worth of leading terms to land on the right
// answer.  This is the single most demanding thing any example in the quant
// set asks of the erfc kernel, and it is why the far-barrier limit checks in
// KAT section 4 use ABSOLUTE rather than relative bands: a relative band around
// 5.4e-27 is not a meaningful statement about an f64 computation whose inputs
// are of order 10.
// ===========================================================================

fn bar_mu(r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64) -> f64 {
    return (r - q - 0.5 * sigma * sigma) / (sigma * sigma);
}

fn rr_a(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64, phi: f64) -> f64 {
    let sigt: f64 = sigma * dm_sqrt(t);
    let mu: f64 = bar_mu(r, q, sigma);
    let x1: f64 = dm_log(s / k) / sigt + (1.0 + mu) * sigt;
    let df_q: f64 = dm_exp(0.0 - q * t);
    let df_r: f64 = dm_exp(0.0 - r * t);
    return phi * s * df_q * dm_norm_cdf(phi * x1) - phi * k * df_r * dm_norm_cdf(phi * x1 - phi * sigt);
}

fn rr_b(s: f64, k: f64, h: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64, phi: f64) -> f64 {
    let sigt: f64 = sigma * dm_sqrt(t);
    let mu: f64 = bar_mu(r, q, sigma);
    let x2: f64 = dm_log(s / h) / sigt + (1.0 + mu) * sigt;
    let df_q: f64 = dm_exp(0.0 - q * t);
    let df_r: f64 = dm_exp(0.0 - r * t);
    return phi * s * df_q * dm_norm_cdf(phi * x2) - phi * k * df_r * dm_norm_cdf(phi * x2 - phi * sigt);
}

fn rr_c(s: f64, k: f64, h: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64, phi: f64, eta: f64) -> f64 {
    let sigt: f64 = sigma * dm_sqrt(t);
    let mu: f64 = bar_mu(r, q, sigma);
    let y1: f64 = dm_log(h * h / (s * k)) / sigt + (1.0 + mu) * sigt;
    let df_q: f64 = dm_exp(0.0 - q * t);
    let df_r: f64 = dm_exp(0.0 - r * t);
    let hs: f64 = h / s;
    let w_s: f64 = dm_powf(hs, 2.0 * (mu + 1.0));
    let w_k: f64 = dm_powf(hs, 2.0 * mu);
    return phi * s * df_q * w_s * dm_norm_cdf(eta * y1) - phi * k * df_r * w_k * dm_norm_cdf(eta * y1 - eta * sigt);
}

fn rr_d(s: f64, k: f64, h: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64, phi: f64, eta: f64) -> f64 {
    let sigt: f64 = sigma * dm_sqrt(t);
    let mu: f64 = bar_mu(r, q, sigma);
    let y2: f64 = dm_log(h / s) / sigt + (1.0 + mu) * sigt;
    let df_q: f64 = dm_exp(0.0 - q * t);
    let df_r: f64 = dm_exp(0.0 - r * t);
    let hs: f64 = h / s;
    let w_s: f64 = dm_powf(hs, 2.0 * (mu + 1.0));
    let w_k: f64 = dm_powf(hs, 2.0 * mu);
    return phi * s * df_q * w_s * dm_norm_cdf(eta * y2) - phi * k * df_r * w_k * dm_norm_cdf(eta * y2 - eta * sigt);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DOMAIN VALIDITY, AND THE FINDING THAT MOTIVATES IT.
//
// The closed forms above price a contract that has NOT YET TOUCHED its barrier.
// That is a precondition of the derivation, not a detail: the method of images
// places an absorbing boundary at H and computes the measure of paths that
// have never reached it.  If the barrier is already on the wrong side of spot
// at inception, there are no such paths and the formula is extrapolating past
// its own construction.
//
// It does not fail loudly when you do this.  Measured at 50 digits during the
// oracle validation for this file:
//     down-and-out call, S=100 H=120  ->  -55.19      (negative price)
//     down-and-in  call, S=100 H=1000 ->  313230.17   (30000x the vanilla)
// and in-out parity held to 1e-49 for both.  A file that checked only parity
// and a handful of well-placed value points would ship this happily.
//
// So the guard is structural: a DOWN barrier requires H < S, an UP barrier
// requires H > S.  Outside that the functions return the canonical qNaN.
//
// WHY NOT RETURN THE ALREADY-KNOCKED VALUE INSTEAD?  A down-and-in call whose
// barrier sits above spot has already knocked in, so arguably it is worth
// exactly the vanilla, and a down-and-out one is worth zero.  That is true and
// it is still the wrong thing to return here, for the same reason
// `examples/quant/black_scholes` returns qNaN for T=0 rather than the intrinsic
// value: this function's contract is "price a live barrier option", and a
// caller who reaches this branch has a state-management bug — they are pricing
// a contract whose status they have not tracked.  Answering the question they
// accidentally asked, with a number that looks exactly like a price, is how
// that bug reaches production.  qNaN says so instead.
// deferred: an already-knocked pricing surface would be a separate, explicitly
// named pair of functions — upgrade path: add `bar_knocked_in_value` /
// `bar_knocked_out_value` returning the vanilla and 0.0 respectively, so the
// caller states which regime they believe they are in and the type signature
// records the claim.
//
// H is also required to be strictly positive and finite: H = 0 is not a
// barrier a price can ever touch, and it makes log(H/S) = -inf.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn bar_base_valid(s: f64, k: f64, h: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> i64 {
    if bs_valid(s, k, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if h != h {
        return 0;
    }
    if h <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if h >= inf() {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}

// A DOWN barrier must sit strictly below spot, else it has already knocked.
fn bar_valid_down(s: f64, k: f64, h: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> i64 {
    if bar_base_valid(s, k, h, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if h >= s {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}

// An UP barrier must sit strictly above spot, else it has already knocked.
fn bar_valid_up(s: f64, k: f64, h: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> i64 {
    if bar_base_valid(s, k, h, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if h <= s {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}

// ===========================================================================
// THE EIGHT ASSEMBLIES.
//
// Each type selects its blocks by whether the strike is above or below the
// barrier, because the payoff region and the knocked region overlap
// differently in the two cases.  The `K > H` / `K <= H` split below is the
// standard Reiner-Rubinstein table, transcribed with phi and eta fixed per
// type.  The transcription was validated at 50 digits against an INDEPENDENT
// derivation (numerical integration of the absorbed transition density of the
// log-price, a completely different derivation path) before any constant in
// the KAT was recorded; see the KAT header.
//
// Sign conventions, fixed here and asserted below:
//     call: phi = +1        put: phi = -1
//     down: eta = +1        up:  eta = -1
// ===========================================================================

// --- DOWN-AND-IN CALL -------------------------------------------------------
// Pays a call if the spot ever falls to H.  With K > H the option can only be
// in the money after having been knocked in from below, so the whole value is
// the reflected block C.
pub fn bar_dic(s: f64, k: f64, h: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if bar_valid_down(s, k, h, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let a: f64 = rr_a(s, k, r, q, sigma, t, 1.0);
    let b: f64 = rr_b(s, k, h, r, q, sigma, t, 1.0);
    let c: f64 = rr_c(s, k, h, r, q, sigma, t, 1.0, 1.0);
    let d: f64 = rr_d(s, k, h, r, q, sigma, t, 1.0, 1.0);
    if k > h {
        return c;
    }
    return a - b + d;
}

// --- DOWN-AND-OUT CALL ------------------------------------------------------
// The Merton (1973) case, and the oldest closed-form barrier price.  Worth
// strictly less than the vanilla call: knocking out can only remove value.
pub fn bar_doc(s: f64, k: f64, h: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if bar_valid_down(s, k, h, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let a: f64 = rr_a(s, k, r, q, sigma, t, 1.0);
    let b: f64 = rr_b(s, k, h, r, q, sigma, t, 1.0);
    let c: f64 = rr_c(s, k, h, r, q, sigma, t, 1.0, 1.0);
    let d: f64 = rr_d(s, k, h, r, q, sigma, t, 1.0, 1.0);
    if k > h {
        return a - c;
    }
    return b - d;
}

// --- UP-AND-IN CALL ---------------------------------------------------------
// Pays a call if spot ever rises to H.  With K > H the barrier is below the
// strike, so any path that finishes in the money has necessarily crossed H:
// the knock-in is certain conditional on the payoff, and the value collapses
// to the vanilla block A.  That degeneracy is asserted in the KAT.
pub fn bar_uic(s: f64, k: f64, h: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if bar_valid_up(s, k, h, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let a: f64 = rr_a(s, k, r, q, sigma, t, 1.0);
    let b: f64 = rr_b(s, k, h, r, q, sigma, t, 1.0);
    let c: f64 = rr_c(s, k, h, r, q, sigma, t, 1.0, 0.0 - 1.0);
    let d: f64 = rr_d(s, k, h, r, q, sigma, t, 1.0, 0.0 - 1.0);
    if k > h {
        return a;
    }
    return b - c + d;
}

// --- UP-AND-OUT CALL --------------------------------------------------------
// The mirror of the above: with K > H every in-the-money path has already
// knocked out, so the option is worth EXACTLY ZERO.  Not approximately zero —
// exactly, as a matter of the payoff's structure rather than of arithmetic.
// The KAT asserts the exact 0.0 rather than a band, because a band here would
// hide a formula that returned a small non-zero number for the wrong reason.
pub fn bar_uoc(s: f64, k: f64, h: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if bar_valid_up(s, k, h, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let a: f64 = rr_a(s, k, r, q, sigma, t, 1.0);
    let b: f64 = rr_b(s, k, h, r, q, sigma, t, 1.0);
    let c: f64 = rr_c(s, k, h, r, q, sigma, t, 1.0, 0.0 - 1.0);
    let d: f64 = rr_d(s, k, h, r, q, sigma, t, 1.0, 0.0 - 1.0);
    if k > h {
        return 0.0;
    }
    return a - b + c - d;
}

// --- DOWN-AND-IN PUT --------------------------------------------------------
// The mirror image of the up-and-in call: with K <= H every in-the-money put
// path has already crossed the down barrier, so the value is the vanilla A.
pub fn bar_dip(s: f64, k: f64, h: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if bar_valid_down(s, k, h, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let a: f64 = rr_a(s, k, r, q, sigma, t, 0.0 - 1.0);
    let b: f64 = rr_b(s, k, h, r, q, sigma, t, 0.0 - 1.0);
    let c: f64 = rr_c(s, k, h, r, q, sigma, t, 0.0 - 1.0, 1.0);
    let d: f64 = rr_d(s, k, h, r, q, sigma, t, 0.0 - 1.0, 1.0);
    if k > h {
        return b - c + d;
    }
    return a;
}

// --- DOWN-AND-OUT PUT -------------------------------------------------------
// With K <= H the put is worth exactly zero: the barrier sits at or above the
// strike, so the option cannot finish in the money without first knocking out.
pub fn bar_dop(s: f64, k: f64, h: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if bar_valid_down(s, k, h, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let a: f64 = rr_a(s, k, r, q, sigma, t, 0.0 - 1.0);
    let b: f64 = rr_b(s, k, h, r, q, sigma, t, 0.0 - 1.0);
    let c: f64 = rr_c(s, k, h, r, q, sigma, t, 0.0 - 1.0, 1.0);
    let d: f64 = rr_d(s, k, h, r, q, sigma, t, 0.0 - 1.0, 1.0);
    if k > h {
        return a - b + c - d;
    }
    return 0.0;
}

// --- UP-AND-IN PUT ----------------------------------------------------------
pub fn bar_uip(s: f64, k: f64, h: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if bar_valid_up(s, k, h, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let a: f64 = rr_a(s, k, r, q, sigma, t, 0.0 - 1.0);
    let b: f64 = rr_b(s, k, h, r, q, sigma, t, 0.0 - 1.0);
    let c: f64 = rr_c(s, k, h, r, q, sigma, t, 0.0 - 1.0, 0.0 - 1.0);
    let d: f64 = rr_d(s, k, h, r, q, sigma, t, 0.0 - 1.0, 0.0 - 1.0);
    if k > h {
        return a - b + d;
    }
    return c;
}

// --- UP-AND-OUT PUT ---------------------------------------------------------
pub fn bar_uop(s: f64, k: f64, h: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if bar_valid_up(s, k, h, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let a: f64 = rr_a(s, k, r, q, sigma, t, 0.0 - 1.0);
    let b: f64 = rr_b(s, k, h, r, q, sigma, t, 0.0 - 1.0);
    let c: f64 = rr_c(s, k, h, r, q, sigma, t, 0.0 - 1.0, 0.0 - 1.0);
    let d: f64 = rr_d(s, k, h, r, q, sigma, t, 0.0 - 1.0, 0.0 - 1.0);
    if k > h {
        return b - d;
    }
    return a - c;
}

// ===========================================================================
// KNOWN-ANSWER TESTS.
//
// THE ORACLE.  Every reference value below was produced by an INDEPENDENT
// 50-digit oracle — mpmath 1.3.0 at `mp.dps = 50`, evaluating the
// Reiner-Rubinstein forms with N(x) = erfc(-x/sqrt(2))/2 in 50-digit
// arithmetic, then rounded ONCE to f64.  Never by this implementation.  A KAT
// generated from the code it tests proves only that the code is unchanged, not
// that it is correct.
//
// THE ORACLE WAS ITSELF VALIDATED AGAINST AN INDEPENDENT DERIVATION, which
// matters more here than in the other quant examples because the eight-way
// (phi, eta, K vs H) table is exactly the kind of thing that is easy to
// transcribe wrongly and then freeze permanently.  Each closed form was
// cross-checked, offline at 50 digits, against a numerical integration of the
// ABSORBED TRANSITION DENSITY of the log-price — the method-of-images density
//     p(x) = [ exp(-(x-nu*T)^2 / 2*sigma^2*T)
//              - exp(2*nu*b/sigma^2) * exp(-(x-2b-nu*T)^2 / 2*sigma^2*T) ]
//            / (sigma*sqrt(2*pi*T))
// integrated against the payoff over the surviving region.  That is a wholly
// different derivation path from the closed form: it shares no algebra with
// Reiner-Rubinstein beyond the underlying model.  Agreement was to 1e-48
// relative or better on down-and-out call (two parameter sets), up-and-out
// call (two parameter sets), and down-and-out put.  Only after that agreement
// were any constants recorded.  None of that integration is in the shipped
// code; it lived in a throwaway oracle script.
//
// FIVE CLASSES OF CHECK, deliberately:
//   1. VALUE       — against the oracle, within a stated ulp band.
//   2. IN-OUT PARITY — knock_in + knock_out == vanilla.  A THEOREM, which
//                    holds independently of the oracle and is violated by
//                    almost every sign error.  The most valuable block here.
//   3. LIMITS      — barrier far from spot: knock-out -> vanilla, knock-in -> 0.
//   4. STRUCTURE   — monotonicity and the no-arbitrage inequalities between
//                    barrier and vanilla prices.
//   5. DOMAIN      — degenerate and already-knocked inputs give canonical qNaN.
// plus 6. ADVERSARIAL negative controls, which is what makes 1-4 mean anything.
//
// WHAT THIS KAT CANNOT CATCH, STATED PLAINLY.
//
// (a) The tolerance bands are structurally blind to any change that perturbs a
//     result by less than the band — including a reassociation of an expression
//     that is algebraically identical and numerically different.  Tightening
//     the bands is not the fix: they must stay wide enough to absorb the honest
//     difference between a re-derived erfc and the 50-digit oracle.  Detecting
//     reassociation requires a BIT-EXACT reference, which is the job of the
//     cross-substrate CI fixture: a hash has no tolerance to hide in.  This KAT
//     proves the pricing is CORRECT; the fixture proves it is REPRODUCIBLE.  A
//     green KAT is not evidence of byte-identity.
//
// (b) THE PARITY CHECK CANNOT DETECT AN OUT-OF-DOMAIN BARRIER.  This is not a
//     hypothetical limitation, it is the measured one that produced the domain
//     guard above: at S=100, H=120, the down-and-out call is -55.19 and the
//     down-and-in call is 65.64, and they sum to the vanilla 10.45 to 49
//     digits.  Parity constrains the SUM of the two halves and says nothing
//     about whether either half is a price.  Section 6 asserts this blindness
//     explicitly rather than leaving it as a comment, so that a future change
//     which "fixes" the domain guard by deleting it is caught by section 5
//     rather than passing section 2.
//
// (c) Nothing here tests a rebate, because a rebate is not implemented (see the
//     file header).  The E and F blocks are absent, not stubbed.
//
// (d) Nothing here tests DISCRETE monitoring.  These are continuously-monitored
//     prices, and a discretely-monitored barrier is materially different — the
//     standard Broadie-Glasserman-Kou barrier shift is a real correction, not a
//     rounding detail.  A caller applying these formulas to a daily-monitored
//     contract is making a modelling error this file cannot see.
// ===========================================================================

fn chk_ulp(got: f64, want: f64, ulps: f64) -> i64 {
    let eps: f64 = 0.0000000000000002220446049250313;
    let tol: f64 = ulps * eps * absf(want);
    if absf(got - want) <= tol {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

fn chk_abs(got: f64, want: f64, abstol: f64) -> i64 {
    if absf(got - want) <= abstol {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

fn is_nan(x: f64) -> i64 {
    if x != x {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

// Assert a strict inequality, counting a violation as one failure.  Written as
// a helper because the same shape recurs a dozen times in section 4 and an
// inline if/else per case would bury the actual claim in punctuation.
fn chk_lt(a: f64, b: f64) -> i64 {
    if a < b {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}

fn chk_ge(a: f64, b: f64) -> i64 {
    if a >= b {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}

pub fn main() -> i64 {
    let mut fails: i64 = 0;

    // =====================================================================
    // 1. VALUE CHECKS against the 50-digit oracle.
    //
    // THE PARAMETER POINTS, AND WHY EACH ONE IS HERE:
    //   D_ATM  S=100 K=100 H=90  r=.05 q=0   sig=.20 T=1    — textbook down.
    //   D_DIV  S=100 K=95  H=90  r=.05 q=.02 sig=.25 T=.5   — the only q != 0
    //          point, so the only one where the dividend drift in `mu` is
    //          exercised at all.  Without it every (H/S)^(2mu) weight is
    //          computed with q = 0 and a dropped q would be invisible.
    //   D_ITMK S=100 K=110 H=90  r=.05 q=0   sig=.20 T=1    — K > H > S-ish;
    //          exercises the `k > h` branch of every down type.
    //   D_NEAR S=100 K=100 H=99  r=.05 q=0   sig=.20 T=1    — barrier 1% away.
    //          The knock-out is 1.367 against a 10.45 vanilla and the
    //          down-and-out PUT is 1.58e-05: near-certain knockout, which is
    //          where the C and D reflection terms nearly cancel A and B.
    //   U_ATM  S=100 K=100 H=120 r=.05 q=0   sig=.20 T=1    — textbook up.
    //   U_WING S=95  K=100 H=110 r=.03 q=.01 sig=.30 T=.75  — OTM strike, high
    //          vol, q != 0, and a barrier between spot and strike.
    //   U_OTMK S=100 K=90  H=120 r=.05 q=0   sig=.20 T=1    — K < H, the other
    //          branch of the up table.
    //   U_NEAR S=100 K=100 H=101 r=.05 q=0   sig=.20 T=1    — barrier 1% away;
    //          up-and-out call is 1.54e-05, five orders below the vanilla.
    // =====================================================================

    // --- D_ATM: S=100 K=100 H=90 r=0.05 q=0 sigma=0.2 T=1 -----------------
    // Oracle doc = 8.665471658245667541555143...
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_dic(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 1.7851119139398992, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_doc(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 8.665471658245668, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_dip(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 5.422305645817085, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_dop(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.15122037643988243, 8192.0));

    // --- D_DIV: q=0.02, sigma=0.25, T=0.5, K=95 ---------------------------
    // The only q != 0 down point; the (H/S)^(2mu) weights carry the dividend.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_dic(100.0, 95.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.02, 0.25, 0.5), 1.7808268716118203, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_doc(100.0, 95.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.02, 0.25, 0.5), 8.611602812379989, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_dip(100.0, 95.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.02, 0.25, 0.5), 4.011536624540996, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_dop(100.0, 95.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.02, 0.25, 0.5), 0.03035132722561154, 16384.0));

    // --- D_ITMK: K=110 > H, exercises the k > h branch of the down table --
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_dic(100.0, 110.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.7439073109286202, 8192.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_doc(100.0, 110.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 5.296180818795616, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_dip(100.0, 110.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 9.620463617003319, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_dop(100.0, 110.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 1.0548612077994586, 8192.0));

    // --- D_NEAR: barrier 1% below spot, near-certain knockout -------------
    // dop = 1.58e-05 against a 5.57 vanilla put: five orders of cancellation.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_dic(100.0, 100.0, 99.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 9.083128722307734, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_doc(100.0, 100.0, 99.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 1.3674548498778336, 16384.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_dop(100.0, 100.0, 99.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.000015826387883905697, 1048576.0));

    // --- U_ATM: S=100 K=100 H=120 r=0.05 q=0 sigma=0.2 T=1 ----------------
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_uic(100.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 9.274518172535203, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_uoc(100.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 1.1760653996503636, 16384.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_uip(100.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.21339815060914935, 16384.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_uop(100.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 5.360127871647818, 4096.0));

    // --- U_WING: S=95 K=100 H=110 r=0.03 q=0.01 sigma=0.3 T=0.75 ----------
    // OTM strike, high vol, q != 0, barrier between spot and strike.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_uic(95.0, 100.0, 110.0, 0.03, 0.01, 0.3, 0.75), 8.200992702840212, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_uoc(95.0, 100.0, 110.0, 0.03, 0.01, 0.3, 0.75), 0.07835181931228669, 65536.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_uip(95.0, 100.0, 110.0, 0.03, 0.01, 0.3, 0.75), 2.253711088251066, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_uop(95.0, 100.0, 110.0, 0.03, 0.01, 0.3, 0.75), 9.510591945416918, 4096.0));

    // --- U_OTMK: K=90 < H=120, the other branch of the up table -----------
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_uic(100.0, 90.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 13.029507938371133, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_uoc(100.0, 90.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 3.6699404700448652, 16384.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_uip(100.0, 90.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.042550479004087975, 65536.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_uop(100.0, 90.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 2.2675461344761705, 4096.0));

    // --- U_NEAR: barrier 1% above spot ------------------------------------
    // uoc = 1.54e-05 against a 10.45 vanilla call: six orders of cancellation,
    // and the tightest thing in this file short of the FAR limits.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_uic(100.0, 100.0, 101.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 10.45056817457969, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_uoc(100.0, 100.0, 101.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.000015397605876996758, 1048576.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_uip(100.0, 100.0, 101.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 4.954133796917194, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bar_uop(100.0, 100.0, 101.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.6193922253397739, 8192.0));

    // =====================================================================
    // 2. IN-OUT PARITY — the headline identity.
    //
    //     knock_in(X) + knock_out(X) == vanilla(X)
    //
    // A theorem, not a calibration: a contract paying if the barrier is touched
    // plus one paying if it is not is the unconditional contract.  It holds for
    // every parameter set, it is independent of the oracle, and it is violated
    // by a swapped eta, a wrong phi, a mis-assembled K-vs-H branch, and any
    // sign error in the A-B+C-D combinations.  This is the block that survives
    // a bad reference.
    //
    // The band is ABSOLUTE and tight (1e-10 on prices of order 10, roughly
    // 1e-11 relative), which is only possible because the vanilla is computed
    // with the SAME kernels — the erfc error is common to both sides and
    // cancels in the residual.  A parity check against an external constant
    // would need a band a thousand times looser and would prove far less.
    //
    // Checked at all eight points, both call and put, both directions: 16
    // independent parity assertions.  Note that each one constrains the pair
    // jointly, so a single assertion catches an error in EITHER half.
    // =====================================================================

    let vc_atm: f64 = bs_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0);
    let vp_atm: f64 = bs_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0);

    // D_ATM, call and put.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_dic(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) + bar_doc(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), vc_atm, 0.0000000001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_dip(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) + bar_dop(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), vp_atm, 0.0000000001));
    // U_ATM, call and put.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_uic(100.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) + bar_uoc(100.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), vc_atm, 0.0000000001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_uip(100.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) + bar_uop(100.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), vp_atm, 0.0000000001));

    // D_DIV — the q != 0 point, so this parity also constrains the dividend
    // drift inside mu and the (H/S)^(2mu) weights.
    let vc_div: f64 = bs_call(100.0, 95.0, 0.05, 0.02, 0.25, 0.5);
    let vp_div: f64 = bs_put(100.0, 95.0, 0.05, 0.02, 0.25, 0.5);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_dic(100.0, 95.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.02, 0.25, 0.5) + bar_doc(100.0, 95.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.02, 0.25, 0.5), vc_div, 0.0000000001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_dip(100.0, 95.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.02, 0.25, 0.5) + bar_dop(100.0, 95.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.02, 0.25, 0.5), vp_div, 0.0000000001));

    // D_ITMK — the k > h branch of the down table.
    let vc_itm: f64 = bs_call(100.0, 110.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0);
    let vp_itm: f64 = bs_put(100.0, 110.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_dic(100.0, 110.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) + bar_doc(100.0, 110.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), vc_itm, 0.0000000001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_dip(100.0, 110.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) + bar_dop(100.0, 110.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), vp_itm, 0.0000000001));

    // U_WING — OTM strike, high vol, q != 0.
    let vc_wing: f64 = bs_call(95.0, 100.0, 0.03, 0.01, 0.3, 0.75);
    let vp_wing: f64 = bs_put(95.0, 100.0, 0.03, 0.01, 0.3, 0.75);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_uic(95.0, 100.0, 110.0, 0.03, 0.01, 0.3, 0.75) + bar_uoc(95.0, 100.0, 110.0, 0.03, 0.01, 0.3, 0.75), vc_wing, 0.0000000001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_uip(95.0, 100.0, 110.0, 0.03, 0.01, 0.3, 0.75) + bar_uop(95.0, 100.0, 110.0, 0.03, 0.01, 0.3, 0.75), vp_wing, 0.0000000001));

    // U_OTMK — K < H, the other branch of the up table.
    let vc_otk: f64 = bs_call(100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0);
    let vp_otk: f64 = bs_put(100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_uic(100.0, 90.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) + bar_uoc(100.0, 90.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), vc_otk, 0.0000000001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_uip(100.0, 90.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) + bar_uop(100.0, 90.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), vp_otk, 0.0000000001));

    // D_NEAR and U_NEAR — the near-barrier regime, where the knock-out half is
    // five to six orders of magnitude below the knock-in half.  Parity here is
    // the strongest single statement in the file: the tiny half has to be
    // RIGHT, not merely small, or the sum misses the vanilla.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_dic(100.0, 100.0, 99.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) + bar_doc(100.0, 100.0, 99.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), vc_atm, 0.0000000001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_dip(100.0, 100.0, 99.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) + bar_dop(100.0, 100.0, 99.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), vp_atm, 0.0000000001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_uic(100.0, 100.0, 101.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) + bar_uoc(100.0, 100.0, 101.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), vc_atm, 0.0000000001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_uip(100.0, 100.0, 101.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) + bar_uop(100.0, 100.0, 101.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), vp_atm, 0.0000000001));

    // POSITIVE CONTROL for the parity block.  Every assertion above would pass
    // vacuously if both halves were zero, or if one half were silently the
    // whole vanilla and the other exactly 0.0 by a short-circuit.  These lines
    // prove the split is REAL at D_ATM: both halves are individually large and
    // neither is the whole.  Without this, "in + out == vanilla" is satisfiable
    // by an implementation that returns the vanilla for `in` and 0.0 for `out`
    // at every parameter point — which is wrong everywhere and passes parity
    // everywhere.
    let di_a: f64 = bar_dic(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0);
    let do_a: f64 = bar_doc(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0);
    fails = fails + chk_lt(0.5, di_a);
    fails = fails + chk_lt(0.5, do_a);
    fails = fails + chk_lt(di_a, vc_atm - 0.5);
    fails = fails + chk_lt(do_a, vc_atm - 0.5);

    // =====================================================================
    // 3. LIMIT BEHAVIOUR: a barrier far from spot is no barrier at all.
    //
    // As H -> 0 for a down barrier (or H -> infinity for an up barrier), the
    // probability of touching it goes to zero, so
    //     knock_out -> vanilla      and      knock_in -> 0.
    // This is a limit, so the checks are ABSOLUTE with a stated tolerance
    // rather than relative: the knock-in values out here are 1e-27 and 1e-117,
    // and a relative band around 1e-117 computed in f64 from terms of order 10
    // is not a meaningful claim.  The honest statement is "indistinguishable
    // from zero at 1e-12", and that is what is asserted.
    //
    // H = 10 against S = 100 is 2.3 standard deviations per year times ten
    // years of drift away; H = 1000 is further still.  These are the most
    // extreme cancellations in the file.
    // =====================================================================

    // DOWN, H = 10: knock-out must recover the vanilla, knock-in must vanish.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_doc(100.0, 100.0, 10.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), vc_atm, 0.000000000001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_dic(100.0, 100.0, 10.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0, 0.000000000001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_dop(100.0, 100.0, 10.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), vp_atm, 0.000000000001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_dip(100.0, 100.0, 10.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0, 0.000000000001));

    // UP, H = 1000: same claim, mirrored.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_uoc(100.0, 100.0, 1000.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), vc_atm, 0.000000000001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_uic(100.0, 100.0, 1000.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0, 0.000000000001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_uop(100.0, 100.0, 1000.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), vp_atm, 0.000000000001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_uip(100.0, 100.0, 1000.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0, 0.000000000001));

    // The limit must be approached MONOTONICALLY, not merely reached.  A
    // formula that returned the vanilla unconditionally would pass every
    // absolute check above; these lines say the barrier still bites at a
    // moderate distance, so "converges to vanilla" is a limit and not a
    // constant function.
    fails = fails + chk_lt(bar_doc(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), bar_doc(100.0, 100.0, 80.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0));
    fails = fails + chk_lt(bar_doc(100.0, 100.0, 80.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), bar_doc(100.0, 100.0, 70.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0));
    fails = fails + chk_lt(bar_doc(100.0, 100.0, 70.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), vc_atm);

    // =====================================================================
    // 4. STRUCTURAL INEQUALITIES.
    //
    // Relations that must hold from the shape of the payoff, independent of
    // both the oracle and the parity identity.  Each is a statement a trader
    // would recognise, and each is violated by a different class of error than
    // parity is.
    // =====================================================================

    // (a) A knock-out is worth strictly less than the vanilla: extinguishing a
    //     contract on some paths can only remove value.  Equivalently the
    //     knock-in is strictly positive.
    fails = fails + chk_lt(bar_doc(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), vc_atm);
    fails = fails + chk_lt(bar_uoc(100.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), vc_atm);
    fails = fails + chk_lt(bar_dop(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), vp_atm);
    fails = fails + chk_lt(bar_uop(100.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), vp_atm);

    // (b) Every one of the eight prices is non-negative.  An option cannot be
    //     worth less than nothing.  This is the check that would have caught
    //     the out-of-domain barrier finding described in the header (doc =
    //     -55.19) had the domain guard not been added — and it is kept as a
    //     standing assertion precisely because parity cannot make it.
    fails = fails + chk_ge(bar_dic(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0);
    fails = fails + chk_ge(bar_doc(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0);
    fails = fails + chk_ge(bar_dip(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0);
    fails = fails + chk_ge(bar_dop(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0);
    fails = fails + chk_ge(bar_uic(100.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0);
    fails = fails + chk_ge(bar_uoc(100.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0);
    fails = fails + chk_ge(bar_uip(100.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0);
    fails = fails + chk_ge(bar_uop(100.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0);
    // The same non-negativity at the near-barrier points, where the knock-out
    // halves are 1e-05 and a sign error would be easiest to miss by eye.
    fails = fails + chk_ge(bar_dop(100.0, 100.0, 99.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0);
    fails = fails + chk_ge(bar_uoc(100.0, 100.0, 101.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0);

    // (c) An up-and-out call is NON-INCREASING IN SPOT near the barrier.  This
    //     is the counter-intuitive one and the reason it is worth asserting: a
    //     vanilla call rises with spot, but an up-and-out call near its barrier
    //     FALLS with spot, because the dominant effect of a higher spot is a
    //     higher probability of being extinguished.  The delta is negative on
    //     a long call.  An implementation with a swapped eta would very likely
    //     get this direction wrong while still satisfying parity.
    //     Oracle values, H=120 K=100: S=110 -> 0.7033, S=114 -> 0.4274,
    //     S=118 -> 0.1402, S=119 -> 0.0695.  Strictly decreasing.
    fails = fails + chk_lt(bar_uoc(114.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), bar_uoc(110.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0));
    fails = fails + chk_lt(bar_uoc(118.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), bar_uoc(114.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0));
    fails = fails + chk_lt(bar_uoc(119.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), bar_uoc(118.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0));
    // And the contrast that makes the claim non-trivial: the VANILLA call over
    // the same spots is strictly INCREASING.  Without this line the decreasing
    // sequence above could be blamed on something generic about the pricer.
    fails = fails + chk_lt(bs_call(110.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), bs_call(114.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0));
    fails = fails + chk_lt(bs_call(114.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), bs_call(118.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0));

    // (d) A knock-in is monotonically INCREASING as the barrier moves toward
    //     spot: a nearer barrier is easier to touch.  For a down-and-in call,
    //     raising H from 70 toward 99 must raise the price.
    fails = fails + chk_lt(bar_dic(100.0, 100.0, 70.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), bar_dic(100.0, 100.0, 80.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0));
    fails = fails + chk_lt(bar_dic(100.0, 100.0, 80.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), bar_dic(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0));
    fails = fails + chk_lt(bar_dic(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), bar_dic(100.0, 100.0, 99.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0));

    // (e) The exact-zero degeneracies.  With K > H an up-and-out call cannot
    //     pay: every in-the-money path has crossed H first.  With K <= H a
    //     down-and-out put cannot pay.  These are EXACT, asserted with a zero
    //     tolerance, because they are facts about the payoff's support rather
    //     than about arithmetic — a small non-zero answer here would mean the
    //     branch structure is wrong, not that the kernel rounded.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_uoc(95.0, 110.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0, 0.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_uoc(100.0, 130.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0, 0.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_dop(105.0, 95.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0, 0.0));
    // ...and their parity consequence: if the knock-out is exactly zero, the
    // knock-in must be exactly the vanilla.  Checked with the tight parity band.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_uic(95.0, 110.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), bs_call(95.0, 110.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0000000001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bar_dip(105.0, 95.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), bs_put(105.0, 95.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.0000000001));

    // =====================================================================
    // 5. DOMAIN CHECKS: degenerate and out-of-domain inputs give qNaN.
    //
    // Not a plausible-looking number.  Zero vol, an expired option, a
    // non-positive spot, strike or barrier are caller errors, and so is a
    // barrier already on the wrong side of spot.  Every one returns the SAME
    // bit pattern — 0x7FF8000000000000, pinned as a literal in the kernel
    // block, because x86 and ARM disagree on the sign bit of a synthesised NaN
    // and a quiet divergence there would break byte-identity on exactly the
    // error path nobody tests.
    // =====================================================================

    // Zero and negative volatility.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_doc(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_dic(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_uoc(100.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_uop(100.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.0 - 0.2, 1.0)));
    // Expired and negative time.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_doc(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_dop(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_uic(100.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_uip(100.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.0 - 1.0)));
    // Non-positive spot and strike.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_doc(0.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_dic(100.0, 0.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_uoc(0.0 - 100.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_uip(100.0, 0.0 - 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    // NON-POSITIVE BARRIER.  H = 0 is not a level a positive price can touch,
    // and it makes log(H/S) = -inf.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_doc(100.0, 100.0, 0.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_dic(100.0, 100.0, 0.0 - 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_dip(100.0, 100.0, 0.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_dop(100.0, 100.0, 0.0 - 1.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));

    // ALREADY-KNOCKED BARRIERS — the finding from the header, asserted.
    // A DOWN barrier at or above spot has already been touched; an UP barrier
    // at or below spot likewise.  These are the inputs that produced -55.19 and
    // 313230.17 at 50 digits while satisfying parity to 1e-49.  All sixteen
    // combinations must be qNaN.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_dic(100.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_doc(100.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_dip(100.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_dop(100.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_dic(100.0, 100.0, 1000.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_doc(100.0, 100.0, 1000.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_uic(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_uoc(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_uip(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_uop(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_uic(100.0, 100.0, 10.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_uoc(100.0, 100.0, 10.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    // The EXACT boundary, H == S.  A barrier exactly at spot is touched at
    // t = 0 by definition, so it is out of domain for both directions.  This is
    // the off-by-one case: a `>` where the code needs `>=` would let it
    // through, and the formula at H == S is not merely inaccurate, it is
    // pricing a contract that has already terminated.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_doc(100.0, 100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_dic(100.0, 100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_uoc(100.0, 100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bar_uic(100.0, 100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));

    // POSITIVE CONTROL for the whole domain block.  Every `is_nan` assertion
    // above passes if the function returns qNaN for EVERY input — including a
    // valid one.  A guard inverted from `h >= s` to `h <= s` would make all of
    // section 5 green and all of sections 1-4 red, but a guard that simply
    // returned qNaN unconditionally would need this line to be caught as such.
    // So: a valid input must NOT be NaN.
    fails = fails + is_nan(bar_doc(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0));
    fails = fails + is_nan(bar_uoc(100.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0));
    fails = fails + is_nan(bar_dip(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0));
    fails = fails + is_nan(bar_uip(100.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0));

    // =====================================================================
    // 6. ADVERSARIAL NEGATIVE CONTROLS.
    //
    // Each line below asks a check to accept something WRONG, and adds the
    // result straight into `fails` — so a spurious acceptance IS a failure.
    // This is the only thing that makes sections 1-4 mean anything: without it,
    // every value check could be passing because the tolerance is wide enough
    // to admit anything, and the file would be a green test that cannot go red.
    // =====================================================================

    // (a) Value bands must reject a reference wrong in the 4th digit.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(bar_doc(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 8.675471658245668, 4096.0);
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(bar_dic(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 1.7951119139398992, 4096.0);
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(bar_uoc(100.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 1.1860653996503636, 16384.0);
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(bar_uop(100.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 5.370127871647818, 4096.0);

    // (b) The near-barrier bands are the widest in the file (2^20 ulps on a
    //     1e-05 quantity).  They must STILL reject a value wrong by 1%, or the
    //     D_NEAR and U_NEAR checks are decorative.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(bar_dop(100.0, 100.0, 99.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.000015984651762744755, 1048576.0);
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(bar_uoc(100.0, 100.0, 101.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.000015551581935766726, 1048576.0);

    // (c) A flush-to-zero near-barrier value must FAIL its band.  This is the
    //     adversarial form of the cancellation claim: it proves the D_NEAR and
    //     U_NEAR checks can actually catch a kernel that loses the number
    //     entirely, rather than passing because a relative band around 1e-05 is
    //     easy to satisfy.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(0.0, 0.000015826387883905697, 1048576.0);
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(0.0, 0.000015397605876996758, 1048576.0);

    // (d) The parity band must reject a vanilla that is wrong by 1e-6 — three
    //     orders above the 1e-10 band but far below anything a human would spot
    //     in a price of 10.45.  Without this the parity block could be passing
    //     on a band so loose that a genuinely broken decomposition slips
    //     through.
    fails = fails + chk_abs(bar_dic(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) + bar_doc(100.0, 100.0, 90.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), vc_atm + 0.000001, 0.0000000001);

    // (e) THE PARITY BLINDNESS CONTROL — the most important line in this
    //     section, and the one that encodes the header's finding as an
    //     executable claim rather than a comment.
    //
    //     Assertion: in-out parity is satisfied EXACTLY by the out-of-domain
    //     barrier values that the domain guard now rejects.  The pair
    //     (-55.19, 65.64) sums to the vanilla 10.4506 to well inside the parity
    //     band, and -55.19 is not a price.  Feeding that sum to the parity
    //     check must therefore be ACCEPTED (returning 1) — and we add
    //     `1 - that` so the line passes when parity is blind, which is the
    //     truth being recorded.
    //
    //     Why assert a limitation rather than silently rely on the guard: if a
    //     future change weakens `bar_valid_down` back to a no-op, section 5
    //     goes red and this line stays green, which correctly localises the
    //     regression to the guard rather than to parity.  Documenting a check's
    //     blind spot in the check suite is how the blind spot stays known.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(0.0 - 55.189440693051736 + 65.6400242652373, vc_atm, 0.0000000001));

    // (f) The monotonicity direction must be REAL.  If the up-and-out call
    //     were increasing in spot near the barrier — the vanilla's direction,
    //     and the sign a swapped eta would most plausibly produce — then this
    //     deliberately reversed comparison would hold.  Asserting that it does
    //     NOT hold is what makes check 4(c) a discriminating test rather than a
    //     coincidence of two nearby numbers.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_lt(bar_uoc(114.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), bar_uoc(118.0, 100.0, 120.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));

    // (g) The exact-zero checks in 4(e) use a ZERO tolerance, so they cannot be
    //     satisfied by a small non-zero number.  Prove that: a value of 1e-300
    //     must be rejected by a zero-tolerance comparison against 0.0.
    fails = fails + chk_abs(0., 0.0, 0.0);

    return fails;
}
